menu "Board Support Package"

    config BSP_ERROR_CHECK
        bool "Enable error check in BSP"
        default y
        help
            Error check assert the application before returning the error code.

    menu "I2C"
        config BSP_I2C_NUM
            int "I2C peripheral index"
            default 0
            range 0 1
            help
                ESP32-S3 has two I2C peripherals, pick the one you want to use.
                The IO expander, touch controller, IMU and RTC all share this bus.

        config BSP_I2C_FAST_MODE
            bool "Enable I2C fast mode"
            default y
            help
                I2C has two speed modes: normal (100kHz) and fast (400kHz).

        config BSP_I2C_CLK_SPEED_HZ
            int
            default 400000 if BSP_I2C_FAST_MODE
            default 100000
    endmenu

    menu "Display"
        config BSP_LCD_RGB_PCLK_MHZ
            int "RGB pixel clock in MHz"
            default 16
            range 8 30
            help
                Pixel clock of the parallel RGB bus. Every frame is read out of PSRAM at this
                rate, continuously, so it trades refresh rate directly against the PSRAM
                bandwidth left for WiFi, LWIP and LVGL. 16 MHz is about 57 Hz. The vendor
                demo's 30 MHz (~107 Hz) saturates PSRAM once network traffic starts, and the
                picture drifts sideways. 18 MHz was marginal on the capsule-radar port.

        config BSP_LCD_RGB_BOUNCE_LINES
            int "RGB bounce buffer height in lines"
            default 20
            range 4 60
            help
                Height of each of the two internal-RAM bounce buffers the RGB scanout DMA reads
                from. Each costs 480 * this * 2 bytes, so 20 lines is 19.2 KB per buffer and
                38.4 KB for the pair. Deeper buffers ride out longer PSRAM stalls but take
                internal RAM from WiFi.

        config BSP_DISPLAY_LVGL_BUF_HEIGHT
            int "LVGL draw buffer height in lines"
            default 12
            range 10 480
            help
                Height of each of the two LVGL draw buffers, in display lines. The buffers live
                in INTERNAL RAM: LVGL blends into them and only the finished strip is copied to
                the PSRAM frame buffer, which keeps blend traffic off the bus the scanout reads
                from. Each costs 480 * this * 2 bytes, so 12 lines is about 11.5 KB per buffer.

                This trades directly against WiFi and the RGB bounce buffers, both of which
                need internal RAM. Raise it only while watching int= and blk= (free
                internal RAM and its largest block) in the application's periodic log line.
    endmenu

endmenu
